<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Hua Guofeng</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1921">1921</a>–<a href="/years/2008">2008</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a> · Also known as Su Zhu, Hua Kuo-feng</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Hua Guofeng was a Chinese politician who succeeded <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mao-zedong">Mao Zedong</a> as chairman of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chinese-communist-party">Chinese Communist Party</a> and also served as premier of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a>. Born in Jiaocheng, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shanxi">Shanxi</a>, he fought as a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guerrilla-warfare">guerrilla</a> in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/second-sino-japanese-war">Second Sino-Japanese War</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chinese-civil-war">Chinese Civil War</a>, rose to lead <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hunan">Hunan</a> province during the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cultural-revolution">Cultural Revolution</a> and became Minister of Public Security in 1973. Mao made him premier and first vice chairman after <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zhou-enlai">Zhou Enlai</a>'s death in January 1976, and in October 1976, a month after Mao's death, Hua had the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gang-of-four">Gang of Four</a> arrested. Still committed to the Maoist line and a command economy, he was gradually forced from power by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/deng-xiaoping">Deng Xiaoping</a> and his supporters between December 1978 and June 1981.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/hua-guofeng.jpg" alt="Hua Guofeng" width="330" height="440" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Sigismond Michalowski · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Musée_de_Bretagne_-_Hua_Guofeng_197904-02_(cropped).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>…24 October 1976, at a rally celebrating the fall of the Gang of Four, the new party chairman <b>Hua Guofeng</b> led the other leaders onto the Tiananmen rostrum to the song, which for a short while served to praise him and was used in newsreels and documentary films.</blockquote><a href="/songs/sailing-the-seas-depends-on-the-helmsman">Sailing the Seas Depends on the Helmsman</a></li></ul></section>
